WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.309 --> 00:00:05.538
We were imprisoned in the tunnel of ice. Ned banged the table with his fist.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:12.459
Conseil stayed quiet while I watched the captain. Once again Nemo's face betrayed no emotion.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.793
"Gentlemen," said Captain Nemo, "there are two ways of dying in this situation."

4
00:00:17.794 --> 00:00:23.197
He made these remarks as calmly as a mathematics professor lecturing his pupils would.

5
00:00:23.198 --> 00:00:28.705
"The first is to be crushed by the ice, and the second is to run out of air."

6
00:00:28.706 --> 00:00:34.856
"We don't have to worry about running out of air, do we?" I asked. "Our reservoirs are full."

7
00:00:34.857 --> 00:00:40.522
"We have spent the last day underwater and already the submarine's air needs to be renewed.

8
00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:49.847
Obviously we can't get to the surface to refresh the air, and in two days—maybe three—we will have exhausted the supply in our reservoirs."

9
00:00:49.848 --> 00:00:54.674
"Well, Captain, can we get out of this situation by then?" I asked.

10
00:00:54.675 --> 00:01:01.301
"We will certainly try," he replied. "I'm going to run the Nautilus aground on the iceberg below us.

11
00:01:01.302 --> 00:01:05.998
My men will then put on their diving suits and attack the ice wherever it's the thinnest."

12
00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:10.919
Captain Nemo left the gallery, and the submarine soon began to sink slowly.

13
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:18.998
"My friends," I said to Conseil and Ned, "we're in serious trouble here, but I rely on your courage and good spirits."

14
00:01:18.999 --> 00:01:27.633
"Professor," replied Ned, "I'm as handy with a pickax as I am with a harpoon, so if I can be useful to the captain, I'm willing to help him."

15
00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:31.641
"He won't refuse your aid," I said. "Come with me, Ned."

16
00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:36.887
I led him to the room where Captain Nemo and some crewmen were putting on their diving suits.

17
00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:40.950
I told the captain of Ned's offer, which the captain accepted.

18
00:01:40.951 --> 00:01:46.117
When Ned was dressed, I returned to the gallery and found Conseil looking out the windows. A

19
00:01:46.118 --> 00:01:50.299
few minutes later we saw a dozen men set foot on the ice. Captain

20
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:57.712
Nemo drilled holes in the ice to determine where it was the thinnest, and then the men attacked the ice with their pickaxes. After

21
00:01:57.713 --> 00:02:01.783
two hours of hard work, Ned came in, exhausted. He

22
00:02:01.784 --> 00:02:06.521
and his comrades were replaced by new workers, including Conseil and me. The

23
00:02:06.522 --> 00:02:12.775
submarine's crew worked for twelve hours and managed to clear away only a small patch of ice. At

24
00:02:12.776 --> 00:02:16.926
this rate it would take us at least four days to get out of the tunnel. "And

25
00:02:16.927 --> 00:02:24.180
even if we do get out of this infernal prison," said Ned, "don't forget that we're still under the iceberg. Who

26
00:02:24.181 --> 00:02:28.943
knows how long it will be until we reach open water and can rise to the surface!" When

27
00:02:28.944 --> 00:02:35.664
I went out to work the next morning, it seemed as if the walls of the tunnel were starting to close in on us. I

28
00:02:35.665 --> 00:02:40.751
didn't say anything to Ned or Conseil, but I did mention it later to the captain. "I'm

29
00:02:40.752 --> 00:02:44.577
aware of that," he replied, as calm as ever. "It's

30
00:02:44.578 --> 00:02:51.744
one more danger, but the only thing to do is remove the ice faster than it is building up on the tunnel walls." I

31
00:02:51.745 --> 00:02:55.902
used my pickax even more vigorously the next time I went out. To

32
00:02:55.903 --> 00:03:03.569
tell the truth, it was a relief to work because the air in my diving suit was now so much better than the air onboard the Nautilus .

33
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:11.474
But as soon as I stepped onto the ice the next day, I noticed that the side walls and the surface below us had visibly thickened with ice.

34
00:03:11.475 --> 00:03:15.228
The submarine would be crushed before we could free it!

35
00:03:15.229 --> 00:03:23.807
I was filled with despair and almost dropped my pickax. I found Captain Nemo and signaled to him that we needed to talk.

36
00:03:23.808 --> 00:03:28.132
We reentered the Nautilus , where we removed our diving

37
00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:36.626
suits. "We must take desperate steps, Professor Aronnax," said Captain Nemo, "or we will be sealed up in this ice as if in

38
00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:41.457
cement. If only my Nautilus were strong enough to bear this pressure without being

39
00:03:41.458 --> 00:03:45.711
crushed." "How much longer will the air last in the reservoirs?" I wanted to

40
00:03:45.712 --> 00:03:49.934
know. The captain looked me in the eye. "They will be empty

41
00:03:49.935 --> 00:03:56.871
tomorrow." Overcome with terror, I broke out into a cold sweat. Captain Nemo, meanwhile, appeared to be thinking

42
00:03:56.872 --> 00:03:59.286
hard. "Boiling water!" he

43
00:03:59.287 --> 00:04:01.617
muttered. "Boiling water?" I

44
00:04:01.618 --> 00:04:05.540
asked. "Yes, Professor Aronnax. We're in a relatively confined

45
00:04:05.541 --> 00:04:11.117
space. Wouldn't pumping boiling water into the sea raise its temperature enough to stop this ice from

46
00:04:11.118 --> 00:04:15.180
forming?" "I suppose so," I said. "Let's try

47
00:04:15.181 --> 00:04:20.006
it." Seawater was constantly being taken onboard and purified for drinking

48
00:04:20.007 --> 00:04:25.744
water. The captain now ordered the crew to heat this water to boiling and pump it back out into the

49
00:04:25.745 --> 00:04:30.569
sea. Slowly the temperature of the water surrounding the submarine began to

50
00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.323
rise. Five hours later the temperature had risen by two

51
00:04:34.324 --> 00:04:40.910
degrees. It continued to rise overnight until it was close to the freezing point—or melting point in this

52
00:04:40.911 --> 00:04:45.236
case. "We won't be crushed by the ice now," said Captain Nemo the next

53
00:04:45.237 --> 00:04:49.648
morning. "And we've made some real progress in removing the ice around the

54
00:04:49.649 --> 00:04:56.902
submarine." But now the reservoirs were out of air. As the day wore on, I felt as if a terrible weight were pressing on

55
00:04:56.903 --> 00:05:01.307
me. It was a relief when it was time to go outside and attack the

56
00:05:01.308 --> 00:05:07.473
ice. Back inside the submarine, I felt myself losing consciousness. Conseil took my hand.

57
00:05:07.474 --> 00:05:12.958
"If only I didn't have to breathe, I could leave more air for you," he told me.

58
00:05:12.959 --> 00:05:15.965
His unselfishness brought tears to my eyes.

59
00:05:15.966 --> 00:05:22.457
"Listen, Master," he said, cocking his head. "It sounds as if the water tanks are filling."

60
00:05:22.458 --> 00:05:27.028
He got up and looked out the windows. "And all the men have come inside."

61
00:05:27.029 --> 00:05:31.528
All of a sudden there was a cracking sound, and I knew what had happened.

62
00:05:31.529 --> 00:05:35.354
"We've broken through the ice below us," I whispered.

63
00:05:35.355 --> 00:05:38.925
"We're free!" Conseil shouted hoarsely.

64
00:05:38.926 --> 00:05:46.020
We had escaped from the tunnel, but how long would it be before we reached open water and could rise to the surface?

65
00:05:46.021 --> 00:05:53.266
After a while I no longer cared. Stretched out on a couch, I felt as if I were going to die.

66
00:05:53.267 --> 00:06:00.584
I could not see or hear or move a muscle, and I have no idea how many hours I spent like that.

67
00:06:00.585 --> 00:06:08.409
Suddenly I revived! Some breaths of air were filling my lungs. Had we risen to the surface of the ocean?

68
00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:14.259
No, Conseil and Ned, my two brave friends, were sacrificing themselves to save me.

69
00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:20.917
A little bit of air remained in the bottom of one breathing apparatus from a diving suit, and they were giving it to me.

70
00:06:20.918 --> 00:06:27.893
I wanted to push the air tank away, but they held it to my face, and for a few moments, I breathed freely.

71
00:06:27.894 --> 00:06:31.409
I looked at the clock and saw that it was eleven o'clock.

72
00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:36.719
Regaining some of my strength, I forced myself to get up and looked at the instruments.

73
00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:45.713
The Nautilus was twisting and turning through the water, moving at a rapid pace, and soon we were only twenty feet below the surface.

74
00:06:45.714 --> 00:06:49.483
But where was Captain Nemo? I wondered desperately.

75
00:06:49.484 --> 00:06:53.683
Were he and his crew even still alive?

